(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["vi"] = {"qs": [["Investor Services", "A CAS covering demat and mutual fund holdings is sent to an investor who had no transactions in the period:", ["Weekly", "Half-yearly", "Never", "Every two years"], 1, "Investors with transactions get a monthly CAS. Those with no transactions still get a CAS every six months showing their holdings."], ["Introduction to Depository", "Which of these laws provides the legal basis for depositories in India?", ["Banking Regulation Act, 1949", "Payment and Settlement Systems Act, 2007", "Indian Partnership Act, 1932", "Depositories Act, 1996"], 3, "The Depositories Act, 1996 enabled electronic holding and transfer of securities and recognised the depository as registered owner, with investors as beneficial owners."], ["Special Services – Debt Instruments & Government Securities", "Government securities held by retail investors in a demat account are kept in RBI's books through:", ["A Constituent SGL account", "The investor's own SGL account with SEBI", "The company's RTA", "The broker's pool account"], 0, "Depositories hold G-secs for their clients in a Constituent Subsidiary General Ledger account with RBI, while investors see holdings in their demat accounts."], ["Special Services – Pledge & Hypothecation", "Corporate benefits such as dividends on pledged shares go to:", ["The pledgor", "The pledgee", "The DP", "The broker"], 0, "The pledgor remains the beneficial owner, so dividends and similar benefits are credited to the pledgor unless agreed otherwise."], ["Investor Services", "Which practice protects a demat account holder?", ["Leaving the DIS booklet with the broker", "Sharing the online password with an agent", "Never signing blank DIS", "Ignoring alerts from the depository"], 2, "Blank signed DIS can be misused to transfer securities. Investors should keep the booklet safe, fill instructions fully and act on alerts."], ["Special Services – Public Offering / Corporate Actions", "For a corporate action, the 'record date' is the date on which:", ["Eligible holders are identified", "The shares are first listed", "The company pays income tax", "Shareholders must vote"], 0, "The depository provides the list of beneficial owners as on the record date; those holders receive the benefit."], ["Investor Services", "E-voting for shareholders of listed companies is facilitated by:", ["The depositories", "SEBI directly", "The RBI", "The broker only"], 0, "Depositories provide e-voting platforms, allowing beneficial owners to vote on resolutions of listed companies through their demat logins."], ["Introduction to the Indian Capital Market", "Which statement about the secondary market is correct?", ["It provides liquidity to investors", "It allows companies to raise fresh capital each time their shares are traded between investors", "It deals only in government securities", "It operates without a regulator"], 0, "The secondary market lets investors buy and sell existing securities, giving liquidity and price discovery. Companies do not receive money from these trades."], ["Special Services – Debt Instruments & Government Securities", "When a bond is redeemed at maturity, the depository system:", ["Converts them to equity shares", "Debits the bonds and pays proceeds to the bank", "Leaves them in the account", "Transfers them to the RTA's account for resale"], 1, "Redemption is a corporate action: bonds are extinguished from the holder's demat account and the principal is paid to the linked bank account."], ["DP Functions – Account Opening", "A demat account for a Hindu Undivided Family is opened in the name of:", ["The Karta, for the HUF", "All adult members of the family jointly as co-holders", "The eldest daughter", "The family's bank"], 0, "An HUF account is opened in the name of the Karta on behalf of the HUF, with the HUF's PAN. Joint holding by family members is not used for HUF accounts."], ["DP Functions – Dematerialisation", "Partly paid-up shares of a company in demat form:", ["Have a separate ISIN", "Share the ISIN of fully paid shares", "Cannot be dematerialised", "Are converted to fully paid on demat"], 0, "Partly paid shares differ in rights and liability, so they are given a separate ISIN. They merge into the fully paid ISIN after calls are paid."], ["Investor Services", "An investor wants to see all securities she holds across NSDL and CDSL accounts in one document. She should rely on the:", ["Delivery Instruction Slip", "Consolidated Account Statement", "Client Master List", "Contract note"], 1, "The CAS, generated by the depositories using PAN, combines holdings across demat accounts and mutual fund folios in one statement."], ["Introduction to Depository", "Securities held in a depository are fungible. This means:", ["Each share keeps its own distinctive number that the investor can trace", "Units of the same ISIN are interchangeable", "They cannot be transferred", "They carry different dividends"], 1, "Dematerialised securities lose distinctive numbers. Any unit of the same ISIN is identical to any other, which simplifies transfer and settlement."], ["Special Services – Public Offering / Corporate Actions", "Credit of shares allotted in an IPO to investors' demat accounts is done through:", ["Off-market transfers by the issuer", "Investors' DPs on their own", "SEBI", "A corporate action by the RTA"], 3, "The issuer's RTA executes a corporate action file with the depository to credit allotted shares to investors' demat accounts."], ["Investor Services", "Which online platform can investors use to file complaints against a DP?", ["UPI", "NPS app", "SCORES", "ASBA"], 2, "SEBI's SCORES platform accepts complaints against intermediaries including DPs, and tracks their resolution."], ["DP Functions – Account Operations", "Before closing a demat account that still holds securities, the client must:", ["Pay tax on all unrealised gains on the holdings", "Sell all securities to the DP", "Transfer the holdings to another own account", "Obtain a court order"], 2, "The client can transfer balances to another demat account in the same name (or rematerialise them). The DP closes the account only after it has no holdings."], ["Special Services – Debt Instruments & Government Securities", "Which statement about T-bills is correct?", ["Pay coupons half-yearly", "Issued at a discount, no coupon", "Issued by companies", "Have a 10-year tenure"], 1, "T-bills are short-term central government securities issued at a discount and redeemed at face value; the difference is the investor's return."], ["DP Functions – Account Operations", "Which facility lets clients submit delivery instructions electronically instead of physical slips?", ["The rights entitlement window", "Rematerialisation", "Pledge closure", "Online instruction facilities"], 3, "Depositories offer internet-based facilities (such as SPEED-e at NSDL and easiest at CDSL) through which clients submit instructions online securely."], ["DP Functions – Account Opening", "Which document explains the rights and obligations of the investor and the DP?", ["The Rights and Obligations document", "The Client Master List of the investor", "The DIS booklet", "The holding statement"], 0, "SEBI prescribes a standard Rights and Obligations document, which the DP must give the client at account opening. It sets out duties of both parties."], ["Introduction to the Indian Capital Market", "Which institution regulates the Indian securities market, including depositories and DPs?", ["Reserve Bank of India", "Ministry of Corporate Affairs", "NSDL", "SEBI"], 3, "SEBI regulates the securities market under the SEBI Act, 1992, including market infrastructure institutions like depositories and intermediaries like DPs."], ["DP Functions – Dematerialisation", "Physical shares in the names 'X and Y' can be dematerialised into:", ["X's sole account only", "Any account opened by Y", "A joint account in the names X and Y in the same order", "The broker's account"], 2, "Names and their order on certificates must match the demat account. If the order differs, transposition-cum-demat is used."], ["Investor Services", "SMS or email alerts for debits from a demat account help investors to:", ["Earn interest on holdings", "Lower taxes", "Avoid KYC", "Detect unauthorised debits quickly"], 3, "Depositories send alerts directly to investors for debits and other key changes, so investors can spot and report any unauthorised transaction promptly."], ["Depository and its Business Partners", "The interface between a depository and an issuer for crediting securities in a corporate action is usually the:", ["Investor's bank", "Stock broker", "Issuer's RTA", "Custodian of an FPI"], 2, "RTAs maintain the issuer's records and electronically instruct the depository to credit securities for allotments, bonuses and other corporate actions."], ["DP Functions – Transmission and Nomination", "All joint holders of a demat account die together. The securities will be transmitted to:", ["The DP", "The government", "The broker", "The nominee"], 3, "The nominee is entitled after all joint holders have died. Joint holders nominate together, and the nominee claims with the death certificates."], ["Foreign Portfolio Investors (FPI)", "FPI registration on behalf of SEBI is granted by:", ["A Designated Depository Participant", "The Reserve Bank of India", "The stock exchange", "The investor's home regulator"], 0, "SEBI has authorised DDPs (large custodians or banks approved by SEBI) to grant FPI registration on its behalf after due diligence."], ["DP Functions – Account Operations", "A DP receives a DIS where the signature differs from the one on record. The DP should:", ["Reject the instruction", "Execute it if the client calls to confirm", "Execute it if the amount is small", "Forward it to SEBI"], 0, "A DP must verify signatures against its records. A mismatch is a ground for rejection, protecting the client against fraudulent debits."], ["Introduction to Depository", "An ISIN is a code that:", ["Uniquely identifies an investor across all depositories and banks", "Identifies a stock broker", "Identifies a bank branch", "Uniquely identifies a security"], 3, "The International Securities Identification Number is a 12-character code that identifies a specific security. Separate ISINs are used for, say, fully paid and partly paid shares of the same company."], ["Foreign Portfolio Investors (FPI)", "Who monitors FPI holdings in listed companies against the prescribed limits?", ["The FPI's home regulator", "The custodian alone", "The stock exchanges' IPF trust", "The depositories"], 3, "Depositories monitor FPI investment limits using holding data and trigger alerts when limits approach, so breaches can be prevented or corrected."], ["Introduction to Depository", "An investor asks her DP which of her papers she can convert into electronic holdings. Which one qualifies?", ["The title deed of her residential flat", "A bank fixed deposit receipt", "Units of a mutual fund", "A life insurance premium receipt"], 2, "Securities such as shares, bonds, mutual fund units and government securities can be held in demat form. Property deeds and FD receipts are not securities."], ["Special Services – Pledge & Hypothecation", "Can a demat holding that is already pledged be sold on the exchange by the client?", ["Yes, without any change", "Not until the pledge is released", "Yes, by the DP", "Only after transmission"], 1, "Pledged securities are under lien. They must be unpledged (or the lender must consent) before they can be delivered against a sale."], ["DP Functions – Account Operations", "A client wants to merge holdings from two of his own demat accounts into one. The appropriate step is to:", ["Ask the RTA to issue new physical certificates", "Rematerialise both holdings", "Invoke a pledge", "Transfer and close one account"], 3, "Consolidation is done by an account transfer instruction from one account to the other in the same name, followed by closure of the empty account."], ["Depository and its Business Partners", "The investor's direct point of contact with the depository system is the:", ["Registrar and Transfer Agent", "Clearing corporation", "Issuer company", "Depository Participant"], 3, "Investors open and operate demat accounts through DPs, who act as agents of the depository. RTAs and clearing corporations interact with the depository, not usually with the investor directly."], ["Foreign Portfolio Investors (FPI)", "SEBI classifies FPIs into how many categories under the current regulations?", ["Three", "Four", "Two", "Five"], 2, "The current SEBI FPI Regulations have two categories, Category I and Category II, based on regulatory status and risk of the entity."], ["Special Services – Debt Instruments & Government Securities", "Interest on a corporate bond held in demat form is paid to:", ["The beneficial owner on record date", "Whoever bought the bond first", "The DP", "The stock exchange"], 0, "The issuer pays interest to beneficial owners as per the depository's list on the record date, crediting their bank accounts."], ["Depository and its Business Partners", "Which partner provides the depository with net settlement obligations of members after trading?", ["The clearing corporation", "The investor's depository participant", "SEBI", "The RTA"], 0, "After trading, the clearing corporation determines obligations of each clearing member and coordinates with depositories to move securities at pay-in and pay-out."], ["DP Functions – Account Opening", "To open a demat account for a company, the DP will require:", ["The personal tax returns of every shareholder of the company", "A board resolution", "A letter from SEBI", "A succession certificate"], 1, "Corporate accounts require a board resolution authorising the account and naming authorised signatories, along with company KYC documents."], ["Foreign Portfolio Investors (FPI)", "A Category I FPI typically includes:", ["Unregulated individuals", "Government and regulated entities", "Indian residents", "Domestic mutual funds"], 1, "Category I covers government-related investors, central banks, pension funds and appropriately regulated entities, which carry lower KYC risk."], ["DP Functions – Transmission and Nomination", "A client wants to change the nominee in her demat account. She can do this:", ["Only once in the life of the account", "Any time by submitting a fresh form", "Only through a court order", "Only with the nominee's consent"], 1, "Nomination can be changed or cancelled at any time by the account holder through a fresh nomination form or the online facility, without the nominee's consent."], ["Special Services – Debt Instruments & Government Securities", "Which of these debt instruments is a zero-coupon instrument by design?", ["A 7-year corporate bond paying 8%", "A floating-rate G-sec", "A perpetual bond", "Commercial paper"], 3, "Commercial paper is issued at a discount and redeemed at face value, with no periodic coupon. The others pay interest periodically."], ["Depository and its Business Partners", "Before its securities can be admitted to a depository, an issuer must:", ["Distribute a dividend in the preceding year", "List on at least two exchanges", "Obtain approval from RBI", "Enter into an agreement with the depository"], 3, "Admission requires a tripartite or bipartite agreement among the issuer, RTA and depository, after which an ISIN is activated for demat holding."], ["Introduction to the Indian Capital Market", "Since April 2019, a transfer of shares of a listed company, other than transmission or transposition, can be done only if the shares are:", ["Held in physical certificates registered with the company's RTA and stamped by a notary", "Pledged with a bank", "In dematerialised form", "Held by a promoter"], 2, "SEBI mandated that transfers of listed securities be processed only in demat form (transmission and transposition were exempted), pushing investors to dematerialise holdings."], ["Special Services – Public Offering / Corporate Actions", "A company announces a 2:5 bonus. An investor holds 500 shares on the record date. The bonus shares credited are:", ["250", "200", "1,250", "100"], 1, "Two bonus shares for every five held: 500 × 2/5 = 200 shares. 250 would be a 1:2 bonus."], ["Special Services – Pledge & Hypothecation", "A borrower repays a loan against shares. To release the shares, the next step is:", ["Pledge invocation", "Transmission", "Transposition", "Pledge closure"], 3, "On repayment, the pledgor requests closure and the pledgee accepts, removing the lien. Invocation is used only on default."], ["DP Functions – Trading and Settlement", "A transfer from a CDSL account to an NSDL account of the same investor is called:", ["A rematerialisation of holdings", "An inter-depository transfer", "A transmission", "A pledge"], 1, "Transfers between accounts in different depositories are inter-depository transfers, routed through a link between NSDL and CDSL."], ["Introduction to Depository", "A depository is similar to a bank mainly because both:", ["Pay interest on the balances held in their accounts every quarter", "Hold assets in accounts and transfer them on instruction", "Lend money to account holders", "Are regulated by RBI"], 1, "A bank holds money and a depository holds securities, each in accounts and each transferring balances on the holder's instruction. Depositories do not pay interest or lend."], ["DP Functions – Dematerialisation", "After the RTA verifies a demat request and confirms it, the depository:", ["Issues new physical certificates", "Credits the investor's demat account", "Sends a refund to the investor", "Locks the shares permanently"], 1, "Once the RTA confirms, the depository credits the electronic balance to the investor's account, and the physical certificates are cancelled."], ["Special Services – Public Offering / Corporate Actions", "In a merger where holders of Company A get shares of Company B, the depository:", ["Asks investors to buy B shares", "Debits A and credits B shares", "Sends physical B certificates", "Keeps both ISINs active"], 1, "Through a corporate action, the RTA debits the old ISIN and credits the new shares in the swap ratio to beneficial owners."], ["Foreign Portfolio Investors (FPI)", "Which entity typically confirms an FPI's trades and settles them?", ["The issuer company", "The FPI's custodian", "A mutual fund", "The RTA"], 1, "Custodians handle settlement for FPIs: they confirm trades executed by brokers and ensure funds and securities pay-in and pay-out."], ["DP Functions – Account Operations", "To prevent misuse, a DP should issue DIS booklets:", ["Only to the account holder on request", "In bulk to the client's broker for convenience", "Pre-signed by the DP official", "Without serial numbers"], 0, "DIS booklets are issued only to account holders on a signed requisition, with pre-printed serial numbers, so that every instruction can be traced."], ["DP Functions – Dematerialisation", "The form an investor submits to the DP along with physical certificates for demat is the:", ["Delivery Instruction Slip with the certificate numbers", "Remat Request Form", "Pledge Request Form", "Dematerialisation Request Form (DRF)"], 3, "The DRF, along with defaced certificates, starts the demat process. A DIS is used for transfers; an RRF for rematerialisation."], ["DP Functions – Dematerialisation", "Before sending physical certificates to the RTA for demat, they are marked:", ["'Cancelled by order of SEBI'", "'Surrendered for Dematerialisation'", "'Duplicate certificate'", "'Pledged'"], 1, "Certificates are defaced with this endorsement so they cannot be misused or traded in physical form while the demat request is processed."], ["Foreign Portfolio Investors (FPI)", "Before an FPI can trade in India, it must have:", ["Indian citizenship for its directors and fund managers", "A listed Indian parent", "PAN, demat and bank accounts in India", "An AMFI registration"], 2, "An FPI needs registration, a PAN, a demat account with a custodian DP and a bank account to settle trades and receive income."], ["DP Functions – Trading and Settlement", "A seller fails to deliver shares at pay-in. The clearing corporation will usually:", ["Cancel all trades of the day", "Ask SEBI to deliver", "Freeze the buyer's account", "Conduct an auction"], 3, "Short delivery is handled through an auction to buy the shares for the buyer; if the auction fails, the trade is closed out at a set price."], ["Introduction to the Indian Capital Market", "Under ASBA, the application money of an IPO investor is:", ["Transferred immediately to the issuer's escrow account and refunded later if shares are not allotted", "Blocked in the investor's bank account until allotment", "Paid in cash to the broker", "Debited from the demat account"], 1, "ASBA (Application Supported by Blocked Amount) blocks funds in the investor's account. Only the allotted amount is debited; the rest is unblocked, so investors do not lose interest on refunds."], ["DP Functions – Trading and Settlement", "Early pay-in of securities by a seller is useful because:", ["It gets the seller a better sale price than the market", "It reduces the seller's margin requirement", "It postpones settlement", "It cancels the trade"], 1, "When a seller delivers securities before the settlement deadline, margin exposure on the sale falls, so the clearing corporation gives margin benefit."], ["DP Functions – Account Operations", "A joint account is held as 'A, B'. The holders want it to be 'B, A'. This change is called:", ["Transmission", "Transposition", "Dematerialisation", "Rematerialisation"], 1, "Transposition changes the order of names. The holders open a new account in the desired order and move securities to it. Transmission applies on the death of a holder."], ["DP Functions – Account Opening", "Which of the following is NOT required to open an individual resident demat account?", ["PAN card details verified with the Income Tax database", "Proof of address", "In-person or video verification", "A recommendation letter from the issuer"], 3, "KYC requires PAN, identity and address proof and in-person or video-based verification. No issuer recommendation is needed."], ["Introduction to the Indian Capital Market", "Which type of instrument gives the holder a fixed claim on interest and principal rather than ownership?", ["An equity share", "A debenture", "A rights entitlement on equity shares", "A sweat equity share"], 1, "Debentures and bonds are debt instruments: the issuer owes interest and principal. Equity shares and their rights entitlements represent ownership."], ["DP Functions – Dematerialisation", "An investor wants physical certificates back for shares held in demat. This process is:", ["Transposition", "Dematerialisation", "Rematerialisation", "Transmission"], 2, "Remat converts electronic holdings into physical certificates, which the RTA prints and dispatches after the depository debits the account."], ["Special Services – Public Offering / Corporate Actions", "In a buyback through the stock exchange tender route, shareholders tender shares by:", ["Sending physical certificates to SEBI", "Transferring shares to the company directly by gift", "Blocking shares via their broker", "Pledging shares to the RTA"], 2, "Tendered shares are blocked or earmarked in the shareholder's demat account through the broker, and are debited if accepted in the buyback."], ["Special Services – Public Offering / Corporate Actions", "Rights entitlements (REs) in a rights issue are:", ["Credited to demat and can be traded", "Sent as physical letters only", "Never transferable", "Credited to the bank account"], 0, "REs are credited in demat form to eligible shareholders. They can be traded on exchanges or renounced off-market, and are used to apply in the issue."], ["Introduction to the Indian Capital Market", "A company issues new shares to the public for the first time. This takes place in the:", ["Secondary market", "Money market", "Primary market", "Derivatives market"], 2, "The primary market is where securities are first issued to raise capital. Subsequent trading between investors happens in the secondary market."], ["DP Functions – Trading and Settlement", "The block mechanism for sell orders means that:", ["Shares are moved to the broker's own account before selling", "Shares are frozen permanently", "Shares are blocked in the seller's own account till settlement", "The DP sells the shares"], 2, "Under the block mechanism, securities meant for delivery are blocked in the client's demat account, then debited directly at pay-in, avoiding transfer to the broker's account."], ["DP Functions – Account Opening", "When opening a demat account, a client who does not want to nominate anyone must:", ["Nominate the DP's branch manager by default", "Submit a declaration opting out", "Close the account after a year", "Pay a penalty"], 1, "SEBI requires either a nomination or an explicit opt-out declaration. This ensures the investor makes a conscious choice about succession."], ["DP Functions – Dematerialisation", "A demat request is rejected because the certificates were reported stolen. The RTA will:", ["Reject it and follow the issuer's procedure", "Credit the shares anyway", "Issue duplicate certificates to the DP", "Transfer them to the broker"], 0, "Reported stolen or fake certificates are rejected, and the RTA follows the issuer's and regulatory procedures, as such securities cannot enter the depository."], ["Special Services – Debt Instruments & Government Securities", "A Certificate of Deposit can be issued by:", ["Retail investors", "Mutual funds", "Scheduled commercial banks", "Stock brokers"], 2, "CDs are money market instruments issued by banks and select financial institutions, and must be issued in demat form."], ["DP Functions – Transmission and Nomination", "Transmission of securities differs from transposition because transmission:", ["Occurs on the death of a holder", "Changes only the order of names", "Requires no documents", "Happens only for physical certificates"], 0, "Transmission transfers securities to nominees, survivors or heirs after a holder's death. Transposition is a change in the order of names among living joint holders."], ["DP Functions – Transmission and Nomination", "Which of these can be a nominee for a demat account?", ["A partnership firm registered under the Partnership Act", "An individual, including a minor", "A private limited company", "A Hindu Undivided Family"], 1, "Only individuals can be nominees. A minor can be nominated, with a guardian named. Bodies corporate, firms and HUFs cannot be nominees."], ["DP Functions – Dematerialisation", "Locked-in shares that are dematerialised will:", ["Become freely tradable from the date of credit", "Be sold automatically", "Lose voting rights", "Remain locked-in until expiry"], 3, "Lock-in status is carried into the demat account with the lock-in expiry date; the shares become free only after the lock-in period ends."], ["Foreign Portfolio Investors (FPI)", "Income such as dividends earned by an FPI on Indian shares is received:", ["In its Indian bank account", "In cash at the stock exchange", "Through the RTA's account", "In gold"], 0, "Dividends are paid to the FPI's designated bank account in India and can then be repatriated after taxes as permitted."], ["DP Functions – Trading and Settlement", "Pay-out of securities to a buying client under current norms is made:", ["Directly to the client's demat account", "Always to the broker's pool account first", "To the RTA", "To the issuer"], 0, "SEBI requires clearing corporations to pay out securities directly to clients' demat accounts, reducing the risk of misuse by brokers."], ["Special Services – Debt Instruments & Government Securities", "Which instrument is issued by state governments?", ["Treasury bill", "Cash Management Bill", "State Development Loan", "Commercial paper"], 2, "SDLs are bonds issued by state governments through RBI. T-bills and CMBs are central government instruments, and CPs are issued by companies."], ["Special Services – Pledge & Hypothecation", "A lender invokes a pledge after the borrower defaults. As a result:", ["Securities are refunded to the pledgor", "The pledge is closed without transfer", "Securities move to the pledgee's account", "The ISIN is cancelled"], 2, "Invocation transfers the pledged securities from the pledgor's account to the pledgee's account, so the lender can recover dues."], ["Special Services – Public Offering / Corporate Actions", "Dividend declared by a company is paid to shareholders':", ["Demat accounts", "Broker accounts", "DP accounts", "Bank accounts"], 3, "Cash benefits are paid to the bank account linked to the demat account. Only securities benefits such as bonus shares are credited to the demat account."], ["Special Services – Pledge & Hypothecation", "In a demat pledge, once the pledge is created, the securities:", ["Move to the pledgee's account immediately", "Stay in the pledgor's account, marked as pledged", "Are rematerialised", "Are sold at market"], 1, "A pledge marks a lien on the securities in the pledgor's account in favour of the pledgee. Ownership remains with the pledgor until invocation."], ["DP Functions – Account Opening", "An NRI wants to hold shares bought on a repatriation basis. The demat account should be linked to:", ["An NRO account", "A resident savings account", "An NRE bank account", "A minor's account"], 2, "Repatriable holdings require an NRE-linked demat account. Non-repatriable holdings use an NRO-linked account."], ["Investor Services", "A depository's Investor Protection Fund is used for:", ["Paying the salaries and bonuses of DP staff members", "Funding IPOs", "Paying market losses", "Investor education and eligible claims"], 3, "The IPF of a depository is used for investor education and awareness and for eligible claims as set out in its rules, not for market losses."], ["Introduction to the Indian Capital Market", "Which of the following is a market infrastructure institution (MII)?", ["A clearing corporation", "A stock broker registered with SEBI", "A mutual fund distributor", "A portfolio manager"], 0, "MIIs are the core systemic institutions of the market: stock exchanges, clearing corporations and depositories. Brokers, distributors and portfolio managers are intermediaries."], ["DP Functions – Account Operations", "A change of address request for a demat account should be processed:", ["On a phone call from the client", "On written request with proof", "Only once in a lifetime", "Without any document"], 1, "Changes of address need a signed request with valid proof, often updated via KRA. This guards against fraudulent changes that could enable misuse."], ["DP Functions – Trading and Settlement", "Under T+1, shares bought on a Tuesday (no holidays) are credited to the buyer's demat account by:", ["Wednesday", "Thursday", "Friday", "The next Monday"], 0, "Under T+1, settlement happens one working day after the trade. Pay-out on Wednesday credits securities to the buyer's demat account the same day."], ["Special Services – Public Offering / Corporate Actions", "A company splits its shares from face value ₹10 to ₹2. An investor held 200 shares. After the split, she holds:", ["400 shares", "40 shares", "1,000 shares", "200 shares"], 2, "Each ₹10 share becomes five ₹2 shares, so 200 × 5 = 1,000 shares. The total face value and the investor's value stay the same."], ["DP Functions – Account Opening", "A demat account for a minor:", ["Can have the minor as a joint holder with a parent", "Can be operated by the minor directly", "Cannot be opened", "Must have the minor as sole holder"], 3, "Minor accounts are held solely by the minor and operated by the guardian. Minors cannot be joint holders."], ["Depository and its Business Partners", "A DP must obtain a certificate of registration from:", ["RBI, as the regulator of payment systems", "SEBI, through the depository", "The stock exchange", "AMFI"], 1, "A DP is registered with SEBI on the recommendation of the depository it wishes to join. It must meet SEBI's eligibility and net worth norms."], ["DP Functions – Trading and Settlement", "A client gives DDPI to the broker. This authorises the broker to:", ["Withdraw the client's bank balance", "Change the client's nominee", "Debit securities for settlement and margin pledges", "Close the demat account"], 2, "DDPI is limited to specified purposes such as settlement of trades, margin pledges and certain transactions, unlike the broader power of attorney used earlier."], ["DP Functions – Transmission and Nomination", "A minor nominee is entitled to securities after the holder's death. Until the minor turns 18, the account will be:", ["Operated by the minor", "Closed", "Operated by the guardian", "Operated by the DP"], 2, "The securities are credited to a demat account in the minor's name, which is operated by the guardian named in the nomination (or appointed) until the minor attains majority."], ["Depository and its Business Partners", "Depositories monitor their DPs mainly through:", ["Daily changes to each DP's share price", "Periodic inspections and audits", "Annual elections among investors", "Reports from mutual funds"], 1, "Depositories inspect DPs periodically and require internal or concurrent audits to check compliance with operational and investor protection norms."], ["DP Functions – Account Opening", "The Basic Services Demat Account (BSDA) is designed for:", ["Corporate clients holding securities worth crores", "Clearing members only", "Small investors with low holdings", "FPIs"], 2, "BSDA offers lower or nil annual maintenance charges for individuals with small holdings and only one demat account across depositories, making demat affordable for small investors."], ["DP Functions – Trading and Settlement", "An investor gifts shares to a relative through the depository. This is a(n):", ["On-market trade through the exchange", "Inter-depository pledge", "Corporate action", "Off-market transfer"], 3, "Gifts are transfers outside the exchange, so they are off-market. The reason must be stated in the instruction, as depositories require."], ["Introduction to Depository", "Which is NOT a benefit of holding securities in demat form?", ["Assured protection against falls in market price", "Elimination of risks like loss, theft and forgery", "Faster settlement of trades", "Automatic credit of bonus shares"], 0, "Demat removes risks linked to physical paper and speeds up settlement and corporate actions. It does not protect the investor from market price declines."], ["Depository and its Business Partners", "Which of the following is eligible to become a DP?", ["A sole proprietor with no SEBI registration", "A listed manufacturing company", "A scheduled commercial bank", "A partnership of retail investors"], 2, "Eligible entities include banks, financial institutions, custodians, stock brokers and certain NBFCs meeting SEBI conditions. Ordinary businesses and unregistered persons are not eligible."], ["Special Services – Debt Instruments & Government Securities", "Retail investors can buy government securities directly from RBI's primary auctions through:", ["SCORES", "MF Utility", "BSE StAR MF", "RBI Retail Direct"], 3, "RBI Retail Direct lets individuals open a gilt securities account with RBI to invest in G-secs directly in primary auctions and secondary trading."], ["Depository and its Business Partners", "An investor's demat account is opened with:", ["A DP of NSDL or CDSL", "The issuer company directly, through its company secretary", "SEBI", "The stock exchange"], 0, "Demat accounts are opened through a DP of either depository. The DP maintains the account on the depository's system."], ["Special Services – Pledge & Hypothecation", "Which party initiates the creation of a pledge in the depository system?", ["The pledgor, through its DP", "The pledgee, without the pledgor's knowledge", "SEBI", "The RTA"], 0, "The pledgor (borrower) instructs its DP to create a pledge; the pledgee then accepts it through its own DP."], ["Special Services – Pledge & Hypothecation", "Under the margin pledge framework, a broker who receives a client's margin pledge may:", ["Sell the shares freely whenever the broker needs funds", "Transfer them to its own account", "Re-pledge to the clearing member or corporation", "Lend them to other clients"], 2, "Brokers can re-pledge client securities to the clearing member and clearing corporation to meet margin needs, keeping securities in the client's account."], ["Foreign Portfolio Investors (FPI)", "An FPI's holding in a company goes above the individual limit. The excess must be:", ["Held indefinitely", "Transferred to SEBI", "Converted into debt", "Divested or reclassified as FDI"], 3, "If the limit is breached, the FPI must either divest the excess within the allowed time or have the investment reclassified as FDI, subject to rules."], ["DP Functions – Transmission and Nomination", "A sole holder dies without a nominee. To claim the securities, the legal heir must generally submit:", ["Documents establishing succession", "Only a copy of the deceased's PAN card", "A new DIS signed by the heir", "A pledge closure form"], 0, "Without a nominee, the claimant must show legal entitlement, through documents such as a succession certificate, probate or, within limits, affidavits and indemnity."], ["Introduction to Depository", "In the depository system, the depository is shown in the issuer's records as the:", ["Beneficial owner entitled to all voting rights and dividends", "Pledgee", "Registered owner", "Nominee"], 2, "The depository is the registered owner of dematerialised securities for transfer purposes, while the investor is the beneficial owner, entitled to all rights and benefits."], ["Introduction to the Indian Capital Market", "A retail investor bidding in an IPO through UPI can block funds using:", ["A physical cheque handed to the registrar with the application form and a copy of PAN", "A demand draft", "Cash at the exchange", "A UPI mandate approved in the UPI app"], 3, "Retail investors can apply in public issues through brokers or apps using a UPI ID. They approve a mandate that blocks funds in their bank account until allotment."], ["Introduction to Depository", "The two depositories operating in India are:", ["NSDL and CDSL", "NSE and BSE", "CCIL and NSCCL", "CAMS and KFintech"], 0, "National Securities Depository Ltd and Central Depository Services (India) Ltd are the two SEBI-registered depositories. NSE and BSE are stock exchanges, and CAMS and KFintech are RTAs."], ["Investor Services", "An investor's complaint against a DP is not resolved by the DP. The next level is:", ["The depository", "The stock exchange", "RBI", "The issuer"], 0, "Investors can escalate unresolved complaints against DPs to the depository, and then to SEBI through SCORES and to online dispute resolution."], ["DP Functions – Transmission and Nomination", "In a sole-holder account with two nominees sharing 60:40, the holder dies. The securities will be transmitted:", ["Entirely to the first nominee, who then shares with the second", "To the legal heirs only", "To both nominees in the 60:40 ratio", "To the DP"], 2, "When multiple nominees are named with specified shares, the securities are transmitted to each nominee in the stated proportion on submission of required documents."], ["Special Services – Pledge & Hypothecation", "Hypothecation differs from pledge in that, in hypothecation:", ["The securities move to the lender's account at once on creation", "No agreement is needed", "Only banks can be lenders", "Invocation needs the hypothecator's confirmation"], 3, "In hypothecation, the lender cannot invoke unilaterally; invocation requires confirmation by the borrower (hypothecator). In a pledge, the pledgee can invoke on default."], ["DP Functions – Transmission and Nomination", "In a joint demat account, the first holder dies. The securities will be:", ["Transmitted to the first holder's nominee", "Frozen permanently", "Sold by the DP", "Transmitted to the surviving holders"], 3, "On the death of one joint holder, holdings pass to the surviving holders. The nominee comes into the picture only after all joint holders have died."], ["DP Functions – Account Operations", "A client wants to stop all debits from his demat account while travelling for three months. He can:", ["Close the account and open a new one later", "Transfer holdings to the DP's pool", "Request a debit freeze", "Pledge them to a friend"], 2, "A freeze can be applied for debits, credits or both, for the whole account or specific ISINs, at the client's request, and lifted later."]]};
